<section class="cs-exec">
  <div class="cs-exec-inner">
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Outdoor gear brands typically carry product ranges organized by activity and season: hiking, camping, climbing, water sports. Demand timing varies across each category. A new product launch, a seasonal campaign, or a category expansion each creates a natural occasion to add campaign coverage. Over several years, that rhythm can produce campaign lists that grow longer than the underlying conversion volume justifies.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">We see this regularly. The campaigns made sense individually when they were added. They just hadn't been evaluated as a system.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">The audit showed an account in exactly that position: more than fourteen active campaigns accumulated over several years, most holding limited conversion data of their own. The client read that volume as comprehensive coverage. The account data suggested it was fragmentation.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">The client associated more campaigns with more control. But most conversions were concentrated in two product groups, while many individual campaigns had relatively little conversion data of their own.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Across the full 2025 calendar year, the account generated a 3.13× blended ROAS.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">We simplified the architecture, activated verified customer data as an additional Performance Max audience signal, and aligned ROAS targets more closely with category margins.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Across April and May 2026, the rebuilt account generated a 4.88× blended ROAS.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-findings">
  <div class="cs-findings-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">Where the account's complexity was working against it</h2>

    <div class="cs-findings-list" role="list">
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">01</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Conversion volume was fragmented across too many campaigns</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">No campaign averaged 20 conversions per week, and 11 averaged fewer than eight. There is no universal conversion threshold at which Smart Bidding suddenly begins working. But in this account, spreading conversion volume across more than 14 campaigns left many individual campaigns with relatively little data of their own. The account had gained complexity without giving each structure enough volume to justify that complexity.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">02</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Most conversions came from only two product groups</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Two product groups generated 83% of purchase conversions. The remaining campaigns divided a much smaller share of the account's conversion volume. That suggested the existing structure was more granular than the underlying demand required. Additional campaign separation was not necessarily creating more useful control, it was fragmenting data across low-volume structures.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Verified customer data was not available as a Performance Max signal</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Performance Max did not have the brand's verified purchaser data available as a first-party audience signal. The client already had customer purchase history that could provide additional context about the people who had actually converted. We connected that data through Customer Match before launching the rebuilt Performance Max structure.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">04</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">One ROAS target was being applied across different margin profiles</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Every product category used the same ROAS target despite meaningful differences in margin. A single blended target can overlook the economics of individual categories. A target that is appropriate for a lower-margin product may be unnecessarily restrictive for a higher-margin product, while a target suitable for a high-margin category may not protect profitability elsewhere. The bidding framework was not fully aligned with the business economics behind each sale.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-mid-hook">
      <p class="cs-mid-hook-text">If your Google Ads account has expanded one campaign at a time over several years, the complexity may still be justified. But each campaign should have a distinct role, meaningful conversion volume, or materially different economics. When those distinctions are unclear, additional campaigns can fragment data without creating more useful control.</p>
      <a href="../growth-diagnostic-landing.html#book" class="cs-mid-hook-link">The Growth Diagnostic maps where account complexity may be limiting performance →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

